WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.597
Let's talk about the individual factors
that influence how risks are perceived.

2
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:09.017
Each person is unique, and this shows
in the way they experience work.

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:12.267
Two people can be in the same team,
with the same tasks,

4
00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:14.507
the same hours and the same managers

5
00:00:14.590 --> 00:00:17.697
and still have completely
different experiences.

6
00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.617
While one feels motivated and calm,

7
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:25.997
the other may feel exhausted,
anxious or on the verge of giving up.

8
00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.237
What explains this difference?

9
00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.867
The answer lies in individual
factors, that is,

10
00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:34.237
everything each person brings
with them into the workplace.

11
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.627
These factors influence how
we interpret situations,

12
00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:42.536
react to stress and use,
or don't use, regulation strategies.

13
00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:45.644
Here are some of the main ones.

14
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:48.857
Adverse life experiences.

15
00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:53.397
People who have been through
difficult situations in their lives,

16
00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:55.337
such as neglect, violence,

17
00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:58.397
grief or poverty,
may be more sensitive to stress.

18
00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.207
Certain situations at work
can trigger emotional

19
00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:04.017
responses linked to past experiences,

20
00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:07.037
even if the current
risk is not equivalent.

21
00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:08.057
For example, a person

22
00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:11.747
who experienced housing
insecurity as a child may react

23
00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:16.207
with high anxiety to a company
restructuring announcement,

24
00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:20.057
even before knowing whether
they will be directly affected.

25
00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.657
Family history and genetic factors.

26
00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:26.467
Mental disorders in the family,
such as depression or anxiety,

27
00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:29.227
can increase individual vulnerability,

28
00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:32.307
especially when there
are no developed coping

29
00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:34.755
strategies or adequate support.

30
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.757
For example, an employee whose
mother had severe depression

31
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.627
may take criticism
at work more personally,

32
00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:45.393
experiencing it as rejection.

33
00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:49.967
Biological factors. Biological
aspects such as hormonal changes,

34
00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:54.077
sleep disorders, chronic illness
or persistent pain directly affect

35
00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.877
the capacity for emotional
regulation and tolerance of stress.

36
00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.077
Our response to work is not only
psychological, it is also physical.

37
00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:05.837
Temperament and psychological
characteristics.

38
00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:08.017
Individual differences in temperament,

39
00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:13.417
such as a greater tendency towards
anxiety, impulsiveness or perfectionism,

40
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.997
shape how each person responds to demands,

41
00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:19.517
deadlines or situations of uncertainty.

42
00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:20.977
For example, a person

43
00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:24.207
with perfectionist traits
may feel great discomfort

44
00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:29.858
in ambiguous settings or where mistakes
are more frequent and inevitable.

45
00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:33.677
Socioeconomic conditions.

46
00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:35.237
Financial instability,

47
00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:38.697
precarious housing
or responsibility for dependants

48
00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:44.097
increase the pressure on the individual
and reduce their room to adapt.

49
00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:48.837
These conditions tend to amplify
the impact of psychosocial risks.

50
00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:49.827
For example, a worker

51
00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:53.647
who depends solely
on their salary to support

52
00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:58.543
their family may tolerate adverse
conditions for fear of losing their job.

53
00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:00.389
Substance use.

54
00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:01.777
The use of alcohol,

55
00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:04.947
medication or other substances
can be a risk factor,

56
00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:08.768
but also an attempt to cope
with stress or overload.

57
00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:12.577
However, when used as the main strategy,

58
00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:18.337
it tends to make the problem worse,
creating cycles of greater vulnerability.

59
00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:19.897
Family and social support.

60
00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:24.077
A strong support network,
whether family, social or professional,

61
00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:27.481
acts as an important protective factor.

62
00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.407
People who feel supported tend

63
00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:33.137
to recover more quickly
from difficult situations.

64
00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:36.007
For example, after a conflict at work,

65
00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:38.387
being able to share the experience

66
00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:43.017
with someone you trust can significantly
reduce the emotional impact.

67
00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:47.027
Physical health.
Health problems such as chronic fatigue,

68
00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:49.477
persistent pain or long-term illness

69
00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:53.647
can undermine the ability
to cope with work demands,

70
00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:55.699
both physical and emotional.

71
00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:01.085
Psychosocial risks
do not act in isolation.

72
00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:03.367
Their impact is deeply influenced

73
00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:06.664
by each person's
individual characteristics.

74
00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:11.117
Promoting healthy working
environments therefore means not

75
00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:12.707
only reducing external risk factors,

76
00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:16.247
but also recognising
the diversity of experiences,

77
00:04:16.330 --> 00:04:19.838
paths and individual needs within teams.
